## Is high blood pressure a disability?

Most people with well-controlled high blood pressure are not affected in their daily life, so it does not usually count as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010** on its own. It may count if it has a **substantial, long-term effect** on your daily life, or if it has led to other conditions such as heart disease, kidney disease or a stroke.

Even so, most employers will help with simple things, such as **time off for appointments**.

## Driving

Car and motorcycle drivers do **not** usually need to tell the DVLA about high blood pressure, unless the condition or its treatment causes symptoms that affect safe driving. The rules are stricter for **bus and lorry drivers**. Check GOV.UK for the current rules.

## Prescriptions

High blood pressure does not give you free prescriptions in England. If you pay for prescriptions and need several items, a **prescription prepayment certificate** can save you money. Prescriptions are **free for everyone in Wales**.

## Benefits

Benefits such as PIP are based on **how your health affects your daily life**, not on having a diagnosis. High blood pressure on its own rarely does this. But if you also have related conditions, such as **heart disease, a stroke, kidney disease or heart failure**, or symptoms such as breathlessness and tiredness, these may affect activities like preparing food, washing or moving around.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you cannot work because of your health, you may be able to get **Universal Credit** or **New Style Employment and Support Allowance**. A free adviser, such as Citizens Advice, can help you check.
